<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  Juno for Mac runs a local coding agent. It spawns `git`, test runners and
  arbitrary approved shell commands, reads and writes whole repositories the user
  points it at, creates worktrees beside them, launches the iOS Simulator and
  built macOS apps, and captures the screen for Computer Use.

  It is therefore **not** sandboxed, and that is a deliberate product decision
  rather than an omission:

  * `com.apple.security.app-sandbox` was previously `true` here. Under the
    sandbox, `files.user-selected.read-write` grants access only to the exact
    paths the user picks through a system open panel, and child processes inherit
    the sandbox — so `git status` in a chosen repository can still fail the moment
    it walks to a parent directory or a sibling worktree, and ScreenCaptureKit is
    unavailable without an entitlement that was never declared. A sandboxed build
    cannot perform this app's core function.
  * It also contradicted the documented security model: `docs/native/TESTING.md`
    records the Mac app as not sandboxed and reasons about its Keychain behaviour
    on that basis.
  * Distribution does not require it. The Mac app ships as a Developer
    ID-signed, notarized and stapled DMG (`docs/native/RELEASE.md`), not through
    the App Store, so the sandbox is not an entry requirement.

  Hardened Runtime is on for the shipping configurations, which is what
  notarization requires, so the two entitlements below are still necessary: under
  Hardened Runtime a process is denied the microphone, and denied sending Apple
  events, unless it declares them — sandbox or no sandbox.

  Nothing here weakens the user-facing gate. Every command the agent runs is
  classified and approval-gated in `JunoCodeCore/CommandClassifier.swift`, and
  screen capture carries a visible indicator with a hard stop.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <!-- Dictation and voice conversations. Required under Hardened Runtime. -->
    <key>com.apple.security.device.audio-input</key>
    <true/>

    <!--
      `osascript` is classified `.critical` by CommandClassifier — the agent may
      run it, but only behind an explicit approval. Hardened Runtime still needs
      this entitlement for the event to be sent at all, and macOS layers its own
      per-target consent prompt on top of it.
    -->
    <key>com.apple.security.automation.apple-events</key>
    <true/>
</dict>
</plist>
